  "content": {
    "id": "p42-q16-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "16",
    "prompt": "Use separation of variables to solve these DEs.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 3,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p42-q16-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{2-y}{x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=\\dfrac{C}{x}+2$, $x\\ne0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1}{2-y}\\,dy&=\\frac{1}{x}\\,dx\\\\\\int\\frac{1}{2-y}\\,dy&=\\int\\frac{1}{x}\\,dx\\\\-\\ln|2-y|&=\\ln|x|+C_1\\\\2-y&=\\frac{C_2}{x}\\\\y&=2+\\frac{C}{x}\\end{align*}$$The constant solution $y=2$ is included by $C=0$. Here $x\\ne0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 43
      },
      {
        "id": "p42-q16-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{xy}{1+x^2}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=A\\sqrt{1+x^2}$, $A\\in\\mathbb R$; equivalently $y^2=C(1+x^2)$ with $C\\ge0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dy&=\\frac{x}{1+x^2}\\,dx\\\\\\int\\frac{1}{y}\\,dy&=\\int\\frac{x}{1+x^2}\\,dx\\\\\\ln|y|&=\\frac12\\ln(1+x^2)+C_1\\\\y&=A\\sqrt{1+x^2}\\\\y^2&=C(1+x^2)\\end{align*}$$Here $A\\in\\mathbb R$ and $C=A^2\\ge0$. The constant solution $y=0$ is included.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 43
      },
      {
        "id": "p42-q16-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=-\\dfrac{2y}{x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=\\dfrac{C}{x^2}$, $x\\ne0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dy&=-\\frac{2}{x}\\,dx\\\\\\int\\frac{1}{y}\\,dy&=-2\\int\\frac{1}{x}\\,dx\\\\\\ln|y|&=-2\\ln|x|+C_1\\\\y&=\\frac{C}{x^2}\\end{align*}$$The constant solution $y=0$ is included by $C=0$. Here $x\\ne0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      },
      {
        "id": "p42-q16-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=y\\sin x$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=Ce^{-\\cos x}$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dy&=\\sin x\\,dx\\\\\\int\\frac{1}{y}\\,dy&=\\int\\sin x\\,dx\\\\\\ln|y|&=-\\cos x+C_1\\\\y&=Ce^{-\\cos x}\\end{align*}$$The constant solution $y=0$ is included by $C=0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p42-q16-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{3y}{x^2}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=Ce^{-3/x}$, $x\\ne0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dy&=\\frac{3}{x^2}\\,dx\\\\\\int\\frac{1}{y}\\,dy&=\\int3x^{-2}\\,dx\\\\\\ln|y|&=-\\frac{3}{x}+C_1\\\\y&=Ce^{-3/x}\\end{align*}$$The constant solution $y=0$ is included by $C=0$. Here $x\\ne0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      },
      {
        "id": "p42-q16-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{y(1-x)}{x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=Cxe^{-x}$, $x\\ne0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dy&=\\left(\\frac{1}{x}-1\\right)dx\\\\\\int\\frac{1}{y}\\,dy&=\\int\\left(\\frac{1}{x}-1\\right)dx\\\\\\ln|y|&=\\ln|x|-x+C_1\\\\y&=Cxe^{-x}\\end{align*}$$The constant solution $y=0$ is included by $C=0$. Here $x\\ne0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 41
      }
    ]
  },
